#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119423 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "19c33c51-bf11-5a81-a0fd-8381d8e0b9d7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/id #uuid "d2cb7f5f-ad04-5e04-b2da-0f9b555442a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-10974" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $y=5+3x$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n2. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n3. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n4. $L$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces:\n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $y=5+3x{:}$\n$y|= 5 + 3x; (0)|= 5 + 3(0); 0|= 5\\cdot$\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ from $L$. Then,\n$a + b|= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 + (-1) \\\\ 5 + 2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$.\nHowever, $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation $y=5+3x$ gives the following:\n$y|= 5 + 3x; 7|= 5 + 3(-1); 7|= 2\\cdot$\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0} \\not\\in L$, this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119424 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83796fe8-f045-5dbc-94d1-6d6e031f3ed8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/id #uuid "d0d76105-c49e-5cf8-af57-bd74989e895b" :question/math-academy-id "e-10977" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ that passes through the points $(1,2,2)$ and $(2,1,1)$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n2. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n3. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n4. $L$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces:\n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n$r|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nfor $t\\in\\mathbb R$, and the corresponding Cartesian equation is\n${x-1}=\\dfrac{y-2}{-1}=\\dfrac{z-2}{-1}$.\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since\n${(0)-1}\\neq\\dfrac{(0)-2}{-1}=\\dfrac{(0)-2}{-1}. \\quad {\\color{red}\\times}$\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nHowever, the point $(3,3,3)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n${(3)-1}\\neq\\dfrac{(3)-2}{-1}=\\dfrac{(3)-2}{-1}. \\quad {\\color{red}\\times}$\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0} \\not\\in L$, this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119425 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f3018fd-9d16-578f-b050-966890844c9b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Plane Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/id #uuid "d2199de1-4bfe-5589-95dd-f9bb7c339437" :question/math-academy-id "e-10980" :question/problem "Let $\\Pi$ be the plane with equation $x+4y-3z=2$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\Pi$ contains the zero-vector\n2. $\\Pi$ is closed under vector addition\n3. $\\Pi$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n4. $\\Pi$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces:\n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $\\Pi$ contains vectors of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The plane does not pass through the origin since\n$x + 4y - 3z|= 2; (0) + 4(0) - 3(0)|= 2; 0|= 2\\cdot$\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Then\n$a + b|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 + 1 \\\\ 0 + 1 \\\\ 0 + 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$,\nHowever, $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ does not belong to $\\Pi$ since\n$x + 4y - 3z|= 2; (3) + 4(1) - 3(1)|= 2; 4|= 2\\cdot$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0} \\not\\in \\Pi$, this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $\\Pi$ is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83796fe8-f045-5dbc-94d1-6d6e031f3ed8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0ffebd0-2f12-5f56-953f-5379674e4a83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116992} {:answer/id #uuid "d5717289-52da-5481-890b-48dfcf4139e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116993} {:answer/id #uuid "3f93f9c7-6486-5fb1-93e9-bd050c94c437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116994} {:answer/id #uuid "5b90bcd4-9e0c-5ee9-a73c-971ed48b7a6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116995} {:answer/id #uuid "74ec59c5-d3a6-5ffd-915b-e0dd9da240f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5717289-52da-5481-890b-48dfcf4139e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" 